[#
  The language profiles: everything the template knows about each managed language that more
  than one file needs (ADR 0006). The facts live in includes/languages.json, one object per
  language keyed by its `languages` id, so shell reads them with jq (`jq -r .go.newest
  includes/languages.json`) and never parses Jinja. This file only decodes them for the
  templates, which import
    [% from 'includes/languages.jinja' import profiles, managed, newest with context %]
  Both files sit outside template/, so Copier never renders them.

  The id is also the language's folder in a multi-language repo, its CI job in ci.yml (the merge
  gate lists `needs` by id), its release-please component and its release.yml outputs. There is
  no separate column for any of those: a new language picks an id that works as all four.

  Fields (all strings unless noted):
    name          display name (AGENTS.md headings)
    newest        the newest known version, Python and Go only: the top of the CI version list.
                  TypeScript tests the Node LTS major the repo picks (node_version) and Rust
                  builds with its pinned toolchain (rust_toolchain), both answers. Raise Go only
                  after the central golangci-lint pin is built with that Go and released:
                  golangci-lint can't lint code for a Go newer than the one it was built with
                  (ADR 0004).
    toolchain     the binary the language's checks and lock need; verify-generated.sh skips the
                  language without it, and the copy-time lock step prints a note instead
    lock          the command that writes the lockfile from the manifest
    registry      where `publishes` sends packages (docs/SETUP.md); null when there is none (Go:
                  the tag is the release)
    release_type  release-please's release-type
    dependabot    {ecosystem, group}: the Dependabot entry for the language's folder
    checks        list of {name, run, fix?, tool?, if?}, in the order to run them. `run` is what
                  CI and verify-generated.sh run; `fix` is the fixing form AGENTS.md shows
                  instead, when there is one. `tool`: verify skips the check when that binary
                  is missing. `if`: {"<question>": "<answer>"}, the check applies only when
                  every listed answer matches.
                  Python's pyright check passes `-p pyproject.toml` because a pyrightconfig.json
                  in any parent folder would otherwise win over the repo's own settings.

  Exports:
    profiles  every managed language's profile, in this file's order
    newest    {language: newest known version}, only for languages that have one
    managed   this repo's languages, in this file's order, each profile plus `id` and `dir`,
              with `checks` already narrowed by `if`. Needs `with context`.

  The JSON is read as a Jinja template before it is decoded, so it must hold no `[[`, `[%` or
  `[#` (no nested arrays).
#]
[% set _raw %][% include 'includes/languages.json' %][% endset %]
[% set profiles = _raw | from_json %]
[% set newest = {} %]
[% set managed = {} %]
[% for id, p in profiles.items() %]
[% if p.newest is defined %]
[% set _ = newest.update({id: p.newest}) %]
[% endif %]
[% if languages is defined and id in languages %]
[% set checks = [] %]
[% for c in p.checks %]
[% set ns = namespace(applies=true) %]
[% for question, answer in (c.if or {}).items() %]
[% if _copier_answers[question] != answer %][% set ns.applies = false %][% endif %]
[% endfor %]
[% if ns.applies %][% set _ = checks.append(c) %][% endif %]
[% endfor %]
[% set entry = dict(p) %]
[% set _ = entry.update({"id": id, "dir": lang_dir[id], "checks": checks}) %]
[% set _ = managed.update({id: entry}) %]
[% endif %]
[% endfor %]
